WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:14.677
A rental project gets built only if its yield on cost, its yearly net income divided by the total cost to build it, beats what an investor could earn buying an existing building by about one to one and a half percentage points.

2
00:00:14.677 --> 00:00:23.159
With Los Angeles apartment buildings selling at yields around five and a half percent in 2026, new projects need roughly six and a half to seven.

3
00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:29.768
Most South Bay projects sit near that line, which is why a single condition can decide whether a project happens.

4
00:00:29.768 --> 00:00:31.500
Total cost has three parts.

5
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:31.821
Land.

6
00:00:31.821 --> 00:00:34.964
Hard costs, meaning the building and its parking.

7
00:00:34.964 --> 00:00:39.325
And soft costs: design, fees, financing, insurance, and contingency.

8
00:00:39.325 --> 00:00:54.014
In Los Angeles, construction runs roughly two hundred seventy to four hundred forty dollars per square foot, and each parking space in a garage costs about fifty thousand dollars above ground and more than seventy thousand below.

9
00:00:54.014 --> 00:00:59.594
Total costs in California routinely exceed half a million dollars per home before land.

10
00:00:59.594 --> 00:01:06.393
Entitled land in Torrance has recently sold for about seventy-six to ninety-two thousand dollars per unit.

11
00:01:06.393 --> 00:01:19.286
And here is the whole problem in one comparison: an existing Torrance apartment building sold in 2024 for about five hundred ninety thousand dollars per unit, close to what it costs to build a new one.

12
00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:24.276
Consider an illustrative project, built from placeholder numbers, not a real proposal.

13
00:01:24.276 --> 00:01:30.485
A hundred apartments, a hundred fifty parking spaces, average rent of thirty-eight hundred dollars a month.

14
00:01:30.485 --> 00:01:39.654
Total cost comes to about fifty-six million dollars, and the yield to about five and a half percent, short of the six and a half or so an investor would need.

15
00:01:39.654 --> 00:01:41.453
Now change one thing at a time.

16
00:01:41.453 --> 00:01:48.764
Requiring the parking Torrance's code calls for, about two point two spaces per unit, drops the yield to about five point two.

17
00:01:48.764 --> 00:01:53.523
A fifteen percent affordable housing requirement drops it to about five point one.

18
00:01:53.523 --> 00:01:59.732
A year of added delay, or twenty thousand dollars more in fees per unit, each cost a few tenths of a point.

19
00:01:59.732 --> 00:02:08.610
On the other side, rents ten percent higher lift it to about six point one, and cutting parking to half a space per unit lifts it to about six point two.

20
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.395
No single change gets the project over the line.

21
00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:17.256
That matches what Berkeley's Terner Center found: it usually takes a combination of changes, not one.

22
00:02:17.256 --> 00:02:21.722
So when an applicant says a condition kills a project, ask concrete questions.

23
00:02:21.722 --> 00:02:24.413
What is your yield on cost with and without it?

24
00:02:24.413 --> 00:02:28.764
What does the condition cost per unit, and how many units does it eliminate?

25
00:02:28.764 --> 00:02:31.055
What did you pay for the land, and when?

26
00:02:31.055 --> 00:02:34.147
Are your construction costs backed by contractor bids?

27
00:02:34.147 --> 00:02:36.952
Are your rents supported by comparable buildings?

28
00:02:36.952 --> 00:02:40.674
Is there a design alternative that meets the condition's purpose?

29
00:02:40.674 --> 00:02:45.712
And will you share your financial model with a reviewer the city hires, at your expense?

30
00:02:45.712 --> 00:02:55.045
Warning signs include a land price far above comparable sales, costs at the top of the range without bids, rents below the market, or a refusal to share the model.

31
00:02:55.045 --> 00:02:56.635
The fiscal side matters too.

32
00:02:56.635 --> 00:03:03.506
Torrance keeps only about twelve cents of each property tax dollar, but it keeps a cent and a half of every taxable sale.

33
00:03:03.506 --> 00:03:07.538
Consumer goods and autos produce nearly sixty percent of its sales tax.

34
00:03:07.538 --> 00:03:19.463
That is why the K Line route fight was really about twenty-eight million dollars a year from car dealerships, and why turning big-box stores into housing has a fiscal cost the city should see before it decides.

35
00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:28.890
For any large change, ask what the site produces in taxes today and after, what services it will add, and whether one-time money is being used to cover ongoing costs.

36
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:34.171
For more, see the development economics and fiscal sections of the Local Official's Handbook.
